WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.459
A worked example: “agents will handle month-long projects within about a year”.

2
00:00:04.739 --> 00:00:19.224
Here is the method applied to a claim of a kind you will hear often in late 2026: that because AI task horizons double every few months, agents will reliably complete month-long software projects within about a year.

3
00:00:19.504 --> 00:00:20.336
Source.

4
00:00:20.616 --> 00:00:26.931
The quantitative basis is METR’s work: the March 2025 paper, the Time Horizon 1.

5
00:00:27.211 --> 00:00:35.232
1 update (29 January 2026), and the Frontier Risk Report (19 May 2026).

6
00:00:35.512 --> 00:00:44.771
The strongest lab-leader version is Amodei’s September 2026 essay, which argues progress sped up from mid-2026.

7
00:00:45.051 --> 00:00:51.557
Note that METR itself does not make the one-year claim; it is an extrapolation others draw from METR’s data.

8
00:00:51.837 --> 00:00:53.267
Rewrite so it can fail.

9
00:00:53.547 --> 00:01:06.837
“By 30 November 2027, an independent evaluator reports a frontier agent with an 80% time horizon of at least 167 hours (one working month) on software tasks.

10
00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:07.415
”.

11
00:01:07.695 --> 00:01:09.188
Access and incentives.

12
00:01:09.468 --> 00:01:22.311
METR has privileged access to labs and states that it took no payment for its 2026 incident investigation; lab leaders have the most access and strong incentives toward short timelines.

13
00:01:22.591 --> 00:01:25.450
Evidence type; measured versus extrapolated.

14
00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:40.535
Measured: on METR’s suite, 50% horizons doubled about every 131 days since 2023; in early 2026 the public frontier was about 12 hours at 50% and about 1.

15
00:01:40.815 --> 00:01:42.586
5 hours at 80%.

16
00:01:42.866 --> 00:01:45.127
Extrapolated: everything after that.

17
00:01:45.407 --> 00:01:50.079
The 80% figure, not the 50% one, is what “reliably” means.
